  "worked_solution": "Let $X$ represent the time it takes to fix a random power outage. From the problem statement, we know that $X∼U[20,160],$ and we wish to compute $P(30<X<60).$\n\nIn general, if $X∼U[a,b],$ then $X$ has the following probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{b-a}, & a\\le x\\le b \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, for our random variable $X∼U[20,160],$ we have the following probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{140}, & 20\\le x\\le 160 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(30<X<60) & ={\\int }_{30}^{60}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{30}^{60}\\frac{1}{140}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\frac{x}{140}|}_{30}^{60} \\\\  & =\\frac{60}{140}-\\frac{30}{140} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{14}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
